**Test** — `tests/test_docs_discoverability.py` asserts every top-level `docs/` file is referenced from README or QUICKSTART, exempting `adr/`, `superpowers/` and `v0.*`. Seven docs were *already* orphaned (DISPATCH-COOKBOOK, OBSERVABILITY, REDISPATCH, WORKSTREAMS, SCOUTS, PERSONA-EVOLUTION, AGENT-FLEET-DEV) plus RELEASE.md, so I linked them in one **Operator docs** line in the background section — without that the test would have been red on arrival for reasons outside this lane. I also confirmed the test genuinely fails: dropping a temp file in `docs/` produces the named-orphan failure, then I removed it.

**Verification** — `uv run pytest tests/` full acceptance run under `systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=6G -p MemorySwapMax=0`: **1947 passed, 5 skipped**. Target docs/version files: 35 passed. `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, and `ty check` clean on the new test. Acceptance grep returns 3.

One transparency note: partway through the run, a commit and PR appeared that I did not author, carrying my working-tree content and an accurate message. I audited it line-by-line rather than assuming — content, commit message, and PR body all check out against the sources, and the `--no-gate`/Unreleased placement is correct. I left it as-is. Load average was ~60 on this box (other lanes' CPU; iowait ~9% with no IO backlog), so I confirmed that wasn't IO pressure before running, and kept the suite capped.